# DURABLE REPRO — the `&`-in-an-owning-position class, DROP facet: a `&`-alias
# in an ARRAY LITERAL element position duplicates the owner's user `Drop`.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg, BOTH backends — C and LLVM agree):
#     1
#     close 9
#     close 9        <- the SAME FileHandle's user `drop` runs TWICE
# The control spelling `[p.fd]` (no sigil) prints `close 9` exactly once, so the
# `&` alone is what duplicates the owner. `ggdef` REJECTS the literal form
# ("`&`-alias in an owning position is not valid"), i.e. the definition lane is
# right and both production backends agree on the wrong answer (Core #8: that is
# two bugs, not a wash).
#
# ROOT (recorded from the filing, verify before acting): the drop-taint reject
# `reject_tainted_formation_arg` is wired only at the two `CallArg` positions
# (`src/semantic/safety/check_expr.rs:116` and `:757`), so a container-literal
# ELEMENT position escapes it entirely.
#
# INTENDED: REJECTED at `gg check`. `Vector[int]` is an owning position and a
# constructor cannot write through, so the `&` is meaningless there — see D10(b)
# ADDENDUM 3 (aggregate construction is an ownership boundary) and D31
# full-strict (`&` means "the callee writes through"). If the program were
# instead accepted with the sigil honoured as a no-op, `close 9` must still
# print exactly ONCE — a single-owner `Drop` violation (D4/D12) is not an
# acceptable outcome under any reading.
#
# The `fd` seed is computed, not a literal, so nothing here const-folds. The
# root is a BARE param (`FH p`), which is what makes the extra owner appear.
#
# Cited from TODO.md (CoW / ownership / High): the `&`-in-an-owning-position
# entry.
struct FH:
    int fd

equip FH with Drop:
    void drop(!self):
        print(f"close {self.fd}")

int seed():
    return 9

void pokep(FH p):
    Vector[int] v = [&p.fd]
    print(v.len())

void main():
    FH f = FH(seed())
    pokep(f)
